0 1 47 16 0 3 invariance of complex exponentials
.TXT 26 -1 26 0
Cg a61.500000,62.000000,201
{\rtf1\ansi \deff0
{\fonttbl
{\f0\fnil Arial;}
{\f1\fnil Symbol;}
{\f2\fnil CG Times;}
}
{\plain {\b \ulnone \f0 Exercise 3.2}{\f0 
  Plot the real and imaginary parts of \{y}{\fs16 
\dn6 \f0 n}{\f0 \} and \{ya}{\fs16 \dn6 
\f0 n}{\f0 \} for various values of  k. 
You should see agreement in every case.}}
}
.TXT 7 0 27 0
Cg a62.375000,62.250000,568
{\rtf1\ansi \deff0
{\fonttbl
{\f0\fnil Arial;}
{\f1\fnil Symbol;}
{\f2\fnil CG Times;}
}
{\plain {This is a remarkable result! Filtering
 a member of this family of complex exponentials
 just results in its multiplication by a
 complex scalar! We say that the members
 of this family are}{\i  }{\b \i0 invariant}{
 under this filtering. The set of complex
 scalars is called the }{\b \i0 (periodic)
 frequency response}{ of the filter. Its
 computation we will call the discrete Fourier
 transform and we will also see that the
 impulse response sequence is recoverable
 from it. Hence this frequency response 
itself will completely determine the filtering
 operation. }}
}
.TXT 16 0 143 0
Cg a62.375000,62.000000,434
{\rtf1\ansi \deff0
{\fonttbl
{\f0\fnil Arial;}
{\f1\fnil Symbol;}
{\f2\fnil CG Times;}
}
{\plain {Therefore, for any input that is
 in the form of a weighted sum of members
 of this family of complex exponentials,
 }{the filter output}{ is easily computed
 by multiplying each term of said sum by
 the filter's frequency response at the 
corresponding frequency. The linearity of
 the filtering operation allows us to do
 this. In effect, the convolution operation
 is replaced by the much simpler operation
 of scalar multiplication. }}
}
.TXT 12 0 144 0
Cg a62.375000,62.125000,712
{\rtf1\ansi \deff0
{\fonttbl
{\f0\fnil Arial;}
{\f1\fnil Symbol;}
{\f2\fnil CG Times;}
}
{\plain {It is therefore of great importance
 to know that }{\ul \i0 any}{ finite sequence
 has a representation as a weighted sum 
of complex exponentials of this specific
 form. This possibility, in a different 
context, was first recognized by Fourier
 and therefore such a representation is 
called a }{\i0 Fourier series}{. Further,
 since the time of Gauss, it has been known
 how to efficiently compute such representations
 with an algorithm now called a Fast Fourier
 Transform (FFT). The existence of the FFT
 has made possible widespread digital signal
 processing }{applications}{. Specifically,
 the periodic convolution, implemented by
 using an FFT algorithm, can be a very efficient
 and practical computation.}}
}
.TXT 21 0 28 0
Cg a60.375000,62.125000,171
{\rtf1\ansi \deff0
{\fonttbl
{\f0\fnil Arial;}
{\f1\fnil Symbol;}
{\f2\fnil CG Times;}
}
{\plain {We shall now elaborate this remarkable
 set of ideas that is central to the  analysis,
 modeling, simulation and }{implementation}{
 of digital signal processing systems.}}
}
.TXT 7 -10 145 0
Cg a27.750000,73.000000,40
{\rtf1\ansi \deff0
{\fonttbl
{\f0\fnil Arial;}
{\f1\fnil Symbol;}
{\f2\fnil CG Times;}
}
{\plain {\b \fs24 Fourier Series for Sequences}}
}
.TXT 4 10 29 0
Cg a62.375000,62.125000,664
{\rtf1\ansi \deff0
{\fonttbl
{\f0\fnil Arial;}
{\f1\fnil Symbol;}
{\f2\fnil CG Times;}
}
{\plain {\i0 Given a sequence of length 
N, we can extend it to have period N. Then
 we may seek a Fourier series type representation
 for the sequence. We may propose as a }{\b 
\i0 basis}{\i0  for such an expansion the
 family of complex exponentials \{\{ec}{\dn8 
\i0 n,}{\dn8 \i0 k}{\i0  = exp[i(2}{\f1 
\i0 p}{\i0 /N)kn], n=0,...,N-1\}, k=0,...,N-1\}.
 We have already observed that }{\i0 \i0 
exp[i(2}{\f1 \i0 p}{\i0 /N)kn] has period
 N in k and that therefore there are only
 N unique such basis sequences. }{Then the
 proposed }{\b \i0 Fourier series representation}{
 of a sequence \{x}{\dn8 n}{, n=0,...,N-1\}
 is of the form of a weighted sum of the
 basis sequences.}}
}
.TXT 27 0 255 0
Cg a62.375000,62.125000,658
{\rtf1\ansi \deff0
{\fonttbl
{\f0\fnil Arial;}
{\f1\fnil Symbol;}
{\f2\fnil CG Times;}
}
{\plain {\i0 It is very helpful to think
 of N-length sequences as vectors in an 
N-dimensional vector space. E.g., visualize
 a three dimensional space, corresponding
 to N equal to 3, and recall how vectors
 could be represented in terms of the (}{\b 
\i0 i,j,k}{\i0 ) unit vectors! The complex
 exponential sequences are, as we soon show,
 an orthogonal set of vectors spanning the
 N-dimensional space. Then any vector in
 the space has a representation as a weighted
 sum of the basis vectors, }{\i0 \i0 with
 the set of weights \{X}{\i0 \i0 \dn8 k}{\i0 
\i0 , k=0,...,N-1\} given by the }{\b \fs20 
\i0 projection}{\i0  of the vector onto 
each of the basis vectors. }}
}
.TXT 17 0 30 0
Cg a57.250000,62.000000,86
{\rtf1\ansi \deff0
{\fonttbl
{\f0\fnil Arial;}
{\f1\fnil Symbol;}
{\f2\fnil CG Times;}
}
